            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for last of all, then the German for the order."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: nachdem",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nachdem — \"after (joining two clauses)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nachdem wir gegessen hatten, gingen wir spazieren. After we had eaten, we went for a walk."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The verb goes to the end, and the earlier event takes the Plusquamperfekt: nachdem er angekommen war."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nachdem joins the preposition nach \"after\" and dem \"that\", so \"after that\". Both pieces are inherited Germanic words; EtymDB lists no parent for the compound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
